[heading Overview]

Boost.Taar is a portable, header-only C++23 library that provides
ergonomic building blocks for writing HTTP servers and clients on top of
__Asio__, __Beast__, __Url__, and __Json__. It is licensed under the
Boost Software License, Version 1.0.

The library is organised around four ideas:

* a [link boost_taar.intro.matchers /matcher DSL/] for selecting which
  handler should serve a given HTTP request,
* a [link boost_taar.intro.handlers /handler/] family that turns ordinary
  callables into HTTP responders, with automatic argument extraction
  from path templates, query parameters, headers, cookies, and request
  bodies,
* a [link boost_taar.intro.session session coroutine] that owns the
  request/response lifecycle for one keep-alive connection,
* a [link boost_taar.intro.server TCP server coroutine] that accepts
  connections and dispatches them to the session.

[heading At a glance]

A complete HTTP server that responds with a JSON sum for path
parameters fits in a handful of lines:

    namespace taar = boost::taar;
    namespace http = boost::beast::http;
    using taar::matcher::method;
    using taar::matcher::target;

    taar::session::http http_session;
    http_session.register_request_handler(
        method == http::verb::get && target == "/api/sum/{a}/{b}",
        taar::handler::rest(
            [](int a, int b)
            {
                return boost::json::value {
                    {"a", a}, {"b", b}, {"result", a + b}
                };
            },
            taar::handler::path_arg("a"),
            taar::handler::path_arg("b")));

    boost::asio::io_context io;
    taar::cancellation_signals signals;
    boost::asio::co_spawn(
        io,
        taar::server::tcp("0.0.0.0", "8080", http_session, signals),
        boost::asio::detached);
    io.run();

The path-template `{a}` and `{b}` placeholders are extracted, parsed to
`int`, and forwarded to the lambda. The returned `boost::json::value` is
serialised as `application/json` automatically. See the
[link boost_taar.quickstart Quick Start] for a fuller walk-through and
the [link boost_taar.examples Examples chapter] for complete programs.

